  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Both ACE inhibitors (angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, ending in -pril like lisinopril) and ARBs (angiotensin II receptor blockers, ending in -sartan like losartan) are top-tier, class I first-line medications for treating high blood pressure .",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Both ACE inhibitors (angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, ending in -pril like lisinopril) and ARBs (angiotensin II receptor blockers, ending in -sartan like losartan) are top-tier, class I first-line medications for treating high blood pressure"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2,
          3
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Effectiveness"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Blood Pressure and Heart Protection: They work equally well at lowering blood pressure and protecting the heart, kidneys, and blood vessels.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              4,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Cardiovascular Outcomes: Large-scale studies show no significant difference between ACE inhibitors and ARBs in preventing heart attacks, strokes, heart failure, or major cardiovascular events.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              6
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Side Effects & Safety Profile"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Side Effect Differences: ARBs have a better tolerability profile and lower rates of side effects.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Dry Cough: ACE inhibitors block the breakdown of bradykinin, which causes a nagging dry cough in about 10% of patients. This happens much less often with ARBs (around 3%).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              8,
              9
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Angioedema: ACE inhibitors carry a higher risk of angioedema (dangerous swelling of the lips, tongue, or throat) compared to ARBs.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6,
              9
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Other Risks: ACE inhibitors have also been linked to higher rates of acute pancreatitis and gastrointestinal bleeding in comparative data.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6,
              10
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Shared Precautions: Both classes can raise blood potassium levels (hyperkalemia) and are strictly contraindicated during pregnancy due to risks to the fetus.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              9,
              11
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Current Guidance"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Major medical guidelines (such as the ACC/AHA and ESC/ESH) list both ACE inhibitors and ARBs as equally valid first-line choices."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "However, growing real-world evidence and clinical preference lean toward starting with an ARB first to avoid side effects like the dry cough or angioedema."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "If you are already taking an ACE inhibitor and tolerating it well, doctors generally do not need to switch you.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              6,
              7,
              12
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like to narrow this down, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Are you or someone you know starting a new prescription or switching from an ACE inhibitor?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Do you have any other conditions like kidney disease, diabetes, or heart failure?"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
